<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kokoda Track campaign</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kokoda Track campaign was fought between July and November 1942 in the Owen Stanley Range of Papua, then an Australian territory. Japanese forces advancing overland towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-moresby">Port Moresby</a> were halted and pushed back by Australian troops, helped by Papuan carriers. The campaign became a defining episode in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s memory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kokoda-track-campaign.jpg" alt="Kokoda Track campaign" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Parer, Damien Peter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Australian_39th_Battalion_after_the_Kokoda_Track_campaign_1942_(AWM_013289).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-singapore">fall of Singapore</a> many Australians feared an invasion of the mainland. The 8th Division had been lost or broken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Ambon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabaul">Rabaul</a>, and Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-curtin">John Curtin</a> brought the 6th and 7th Divisions home from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>'s wish to send them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. Following the Battle of the Coral Sea, the Japanese 17th Army at Rabaul planned to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-moresby">Port Moresby</a> overland, relying on pre-war reports of a road from Kokoda. The task went to the South Seas Detachment under Major General Tomitarō Horii, who doubted it could be supplied but did not press his objection.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Japanese troops landed near Gona on the evening of 21 July 1942 and pushed inland against Maroubra Force, made up of four platoons of the 39th Battalion and men of the Papuan Infantry Battalion. Kokoda and its airfield fell on 29 July, and the 39th Battalion's commander, Owen, was mortally wounded. The Australians were driven back through Isurava and Eora Creek, and the 21st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> narrowly escaped capture at Mission Ridge and Brigade Hill from 6 to 8 September. Brigadier Kenneth Eather's 25th Brigade halted the advance at Ioribaiwa and withdrew to Imita Ridge.</p><p>Within sight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-moresby">Port Moresby</a>, the Japanese, who had outrun their supplies and were ordered back after reverses at Guadalcanal, withdrew on 26 September. Pursuing Australians met strong prepared positions at Templeton's Crossing and Eora Village from 11 to 28 October, reoccupied Kokoda unopposed and won the battle around Oivi and Gorari from 4 to 11 November. Horii drowned in the flooded Kumusi River, and by 16 November two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> of the 7th Division had crossed it at Wairopi to advance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> troops on the beachheads.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Japanese at Buna–Gona held out until 22 January 1943. About 6,000 Japanese fought in the forward areas of the track, and all Australians captured in the campaign were executed. The jungle fighting led to wide changes in Australian doctrine, training and equipment, and a crisis of command under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur">Douglas MacArthur</a> and General Sir Thomas Blamey cost three senior officers their posts. Damien Parer's newsreel Kokoda Front Line! won an Oscar, and the campaign entered the Anzac legend as a battle that saved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, though the idea that the Australians were far outnumbered has since been shown to be wrong.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kokoda Track campaign” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Japan entered the war, Singapore fell in February 1942 with the capture of most of the 8th Division, Darwin was bombed, and Australian troops halted the Japanese advance on the <b>Kokoda Track</b> in New Guinea, while Prime Minister John Curtin turned to the United States for protection.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Japanese advance on Port Moresby was stopped on the <b>Kokoda Track</b> in 1942 with help from Papuan carriers.</blockquote><a href="/state/territory-of-papua">Territory of Papua</a></li></ul></section>
